p.src:
Revision enters next, and here Macfarlane gives two of the lecture’s strongest practical models. The first is the potter model: $\begin{aligned}
\text{wet mass of clay} &\longrightarrow \text{spin} \longrightarrow \text{shape} \longrightarrow \text{ornament},\\
\text{raw draft} &\longrightarrow \text{repeated passes} \longrightarrow \text{form} \longrightarrow \text{finish}.\end{aligned}$ The point is not decorative analogy. It is a refusal of the fantasy that finished prose arrives clean. The initial material is muddy. Form emerges through repeated shaping.

